## Why This Is Asked
Real-time trading platforms demand ultra-low latency combined with robust replay protection across distributed microservices. Token binding mechanisms reduce impersonation risks while maintaining performance requirements critical to trading operations.

## Key Concepts
- Token binding and mutual TLS (mTLS) for connection-specific authentication
- Short time-to-live (TTL) tokens with audience-scoping for service isolation
- WebSocket connection nonces and replay prevention mechanisms
- Global revocation propagation strategies
- Comprehensive observability and audit trail implementation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: validate token and bind to nonce
function authorize(token, nonce, ctx){
  if (!isValidToken(token)) return false;
  if (!bindsToTLS(token, ctx)) return false;
  const session = createSecureSession(token, nonce);
  return session.isValid() && !isReplayAttempt(nonce);
}
```

## Concept Overview
JWT authentication with RS256 provides asymmetric cryptography for enhanced security. Refresh token rotation prevents replay attacks by generating new refresh tokens on each use.

## Implementation Details
- **RS256 Algorithm**: Uses private/public key pair instead of shared secret
- **Access Token**: 15-minute expiration, contains user ID and role
- **Refresh Token**: 7-day expiration, stored in httpOnly secure cookie
- **Token Rotation**: New refresh token issued each time, old one invalidated

## Code Example
```javascript
// Generate JWT with RS256
const token = jwt.sign(
  { userId: user.id, role: user.role },
  privateKey,
  { algorithm: 'RS256', expiresIn: '15m' }
);

// Refresh token rotation
app.post('/refresh', async (req, res) => {
  const oldRefreshToken = req.cookies.refresh_token;
  const newTokens = await generateNewTokens(oldRefreshToken);
  await invalidateRefreshToken(oldRefreshToken);
  res.cookie('refresh_token', newTokens.refreshToken, {
    httpOnly: true, secure: true, sameSite: 'strict'
  });
});
```

## Common Pitfalls
- Using HS256 instead of RS256 (shared secret vulnerability)
- Storing refresh tokens in localStorage (XSS risk)
- Not implementing proper token rotation
- Missing secure cookie flags
- Forgetting to invalidate old refresh tokens

## Why This Is Asked
Assess practical webhook security basics: replay protection, signatures, and minimal state.

## Key Concepts
- Webhook signing and HMAC
- Idempotency keys and nonce tracking
- Replay window and TTL
- Secret rotation and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: verify HMAC and replay
const crypto = require('crypto');
function verify(payload, signature, secret, nonce, redis) {
  const h = crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret).update(payload).digest('hex');
  if (h !== signature) return false;
  // idempotency: ensure nonce not seen
  const seen = redis.setnx(`nonce:${nonce}`, '1');
  if (!seen) return false;
  redis.expire(`nonce:${nonce}`, 300);
  return true;
}
```

